        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nI've encountered similar request for Neovim's built-in commenting and it didn't sound worth adding.\n\nAs far as I can tell, the only valid use case for \"inner comment\" is indeed \"change a comment\": i.e. type `cic` and the whole current comment is \"collapsed\" to the point that there is a single commented blank line in Insert mode. There is no common and good use for anything else:\n- Yanking with `yic` won't work as expected: yanked text will be as the whole comment but without left comment part on the first line and right comment part on the last. I'd expect the use case here to be yank *uncommented* text.\n- Deleting with `dic` will result in a commented blank line, which I don't think is a good outcome to allow.\n- Maybe something like `guic` / `gUic` / `g~ic` would work (i.e. make comment text lowercase / uppercase / othercase), but I don't think it is *that* common/useful.\n\nHave I understood the suggestions about \"inner comment\" and \"outer comment\" correctly here? Does the above make sense?",
