            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Yeah, that's all understandable. Do you know what the custom textobject for an \"inner comment* might look like? If I could define it myself via `mini.ai`, I would be happy with that - it wouldn't be default behavior, but those who wanted it could add it themselves\n\nFor 'mini.ai' - probably something like a function that traverses lines of the whole buffer and computes all possible comment blocks according to the definition of \"inner\": charwise, excluding left comment leader on first line, excluding right comment leader on last line. This, for example, would allow for something like `cinc` (i.e. \"change inside *next* comment\") while being explicit.\n\nA more concise solution might be to utilize `MiniComment.textobject()` which powers the `gc` textobject (by selecting its region linewise). So something like this:\n\n```lua\nlocal select_inner_comment = function()\n  -- Try selecting comment block as \"whole lines\"\n  MiniComment.textobject()\n  if vim.fn.mode() ~= 'V' then return end\n\n  -- Adjust selection to be charwise and not include edge comment parts\n  local comment_left, comment_right = vim.bo.commentstring:match('^(.*)%%s(.*)$')\n  if comment_left == nil then return end\n\n  -- NOTE: this depends on implementation detail that `textobject` puts\n  -- cursor on last line of comment block\n  local from_line, to_line = vim.fn.line('v'), vim.fn.line('.')\n\n  vim.fn.feedkeys('v', 'nx')\n  local to_col = vim.fn.getline(to_line):find(vim.pesc(comment_right) .. '%s*$')\n  vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { to_line, to_col - 2 })\n\n  vim.fn.feedkeys('o', 'nx')\n  local _, from_col = vim.fn.getline(from_line):find('^%s*' .. vim.pesc(comment_left))\n  vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { from_line, from_col })\n  vim.fn.feedkeys('o', 'nx')\nend\n\nvim.keymap.set({ 'x', 'o' }, 'ic', select_inner_comment)\n```\n\nI'll warn that `MiniComment.textobject()` is not really meant for public use and *might* change behavior in the future (or be removed completely). However, the actual probability here is not that high.",
